conditions including wind. Failures and external conditions that lead to structural defects are not considered in this paper since their impact on TR operation is obvious. The situation is more complicated with aerodynamic effects that can have a negative impact on TR and helicopter integrally. Literature analysis on the unanticipated yaw of a single-rotor helicopter suggests that there are three main reasons for this phenomenon:
• TR thrust reduction because of MR and TR interference. • Aerodynamic yaw moment occurrence when blowing the helicopter from behind because of its directional stability. • TR thrust reduction because of the vortex ring state.
In most papers dedicated to this phenomenon [1–12], it is considered that the main reason that triggers unanticipated yaw is the well-known vortex ring state which the TR falls into when it is blown by a side wind or when it turns sharply on the yaw. Vortex ring state occurs when TR is blown by airflow against the throwback flow (flow ejected by TR). If the airflow velocity is equal in value and opposite in direction to the TR throwback velocity (or ejection velocity), then the
