allow varying the angle β between propeller axis of rotation and airflow velocity vector. It could give an opportunity to imitate tail rotor side-blowing while varying helicopter sideslip angle. During the experiment, the angle between propeller axis of rotation and airflow velocity vector varied from 0 to 90 degrees with a step of 10°. Airflow velocity V 0 generated by wind tunnel varied from 2 to 10 m/s with a step of 1 m/s. Propeller was blown for increasing blades angles of attack (against the direction of induced velocity, V < 0) and for decreasing blades angles of attack (in the direction of induced velocity, V > 0). Electric motor input power was 16.1 W, in these conditions, propeller rotation speed varied from 5410 to 6280 rpm because of airflow affecting. Propeller rotation speed was measured by the laser digital tachometer. Propeller thrust without airflow blowing was 1.12 N with rotation speed of 5517 rpm. Thrust value was defined by extracting electric motor drag from the force measured by mechanical scales. Propeller throwback velocity was about 6 m/s, it was measured by the anemometer. Wind tunnel experiment was carried out as follows. Airflow velocity was set, electric motor was switched on, propeller gained speed, propeller thrust and rotation speed were measured. Then, the angle between propeller axis of rotation and airflow velocity vector was varied by rotating the scales base. Propeller thrust and rotation speed were measured again. Results of measurements are presented and analyzed below.
3 Experiments results and analysis
Figure 3 below shows graphs of dependencies of propeller thrust T on airflow velocity V at different angles β between propeller axis of rotation and airflow velocity vector. It can be seen from the presented graphs that at low angles β and positive airflow velocity the propeller thrust decreases while velocity increases. This is due to propeller blades sections angles of attack decrease. At negative airflow velocity, the propeller thrust increases while velocity absolute value increases. This is due to propeller blades sections angles of attack increase. The general character of the graph at β 0 corresponds to the graphs given in [22] (Fig. 4). In these graphs, the dependencies of relative thrust ct on relative airflow velocity V 0 at different propeller pitch angles φ are provided. However, in Fig. 3, in the certain range of negative velocities, there is a drop of thrust with a minimum at about V 6 m/s. This velocity corresponds to the propeller throwback velocity measured earlier. In these blowing conditions, the vortex ring state forms. Therefore, there is a thrust drop at the vortex ring state. Nevertheless as seen from the graphs, the propeller thrust does not drop to zero
in these conditions, although according to earlier mentioned impulse theory there should be no propeller thrust. This conflict statement was mentioned by the author of the propeller impulse theory Yuriev as he noted that the impulse theory is not applicable to the vortex ring state [22]. Meanwhile, there is no thrust drop on the graphs provided in his work at all (Fig. 4). It seems that the presence or absence of thrust drop depends on propeller type (with hinged or rigid blades attachments), its aerodynamic and geometric characteristics. These dependencies should be clarified in future works. In further considerations, let us rely on the fact that the thrust drop at the vortex ring state still exists. Therefore, the question arises whether the experimentally obtained thrust drop can cause helicopter unanticipated yaw rotation? Let the helicopter be balanced with no crosswind. With crosswind appearance and its velocity, gradual increase in the direction opposite to the propeller throwback velocity first the TR thrust will increase up to the velocity V 4 m/s. Then, it will decrease to a minimum at velocity V 6 m/s but the thrust in these conditions will be almost equal to the initial thrust without crosswind blowing. After that with a further wind velocity increase, the propeller thrust will start to increase again. In these conditions, with no pilot control input helicopter will yaw to the direction opposite to the unanticipated yaw rotation (for European-made helicopters the unanticipated yaw happens to the left, for US-made helicopters—to the right; it is determined by MR rotation direction and its torque which is compensated by the TR). It should be mentioned that as can be seen in Fig. 3 the thrust drop is eliminated at β more than 40°. If the helicopter is balanced with crosswind velocity V 4 m/s then with wind velocity increase the thrust will decrease which can generally lead to the start of unanticipated yaw rotation. But it should be mentioned that in these conditions helicopter airframe aerodynamic yaw moment My ha will increase as well and it will prevent helicopter rotation (Fig. 5; also shown: My rMR—main rotor torque, My TR—TR thrust moment, T TR—TR thrust). It is also possible that tail rotor thrust decreases due to the decrease of TR rotation speed because of required power increase above available power [10]. However, when helicopter rotates on yaw from the position shown in Fig. 5 airflow blowing becomes side-blowing instead of axial blowing and vortex ring state eliminates when β exceeds 40°. The power required for TR rotation should significantly decrease and its rotation speed should be restored. However, this is a speculative conclusion that requires deeper study and additional research. Attention should be also paid to the real unanticipated yaw rotation events when the helicopter angular yaw speed rapidly increases up to 100 °/s and more (in some cases, it reached 165 °/s [21]). Authors of this paper think that this can happen if only TR thrust decreases dramatically almost to zero and
