4 Conclusion
Above presented experiments with a propeller in a wind tunnel show that when the propeller is blown for increasing blades sections angles of attack (in the direction of propeller thrust vector), the propeller thrust generally increases. But in the range of angles between propeller axis of rotation and airflow velocity vector from 0° to 40°, the vortex ring state occurs which causes a thrust drop at airflow velocities from about 4 to 7 m/s. Under certain conditions, this thrust drop can cause the unanticipated yaw rotation. However, according to the authors, this thrust drop is insignificant which nevertheless can increase due to the tail rotor rotation speed decrease on a real helicopter (because of the increase of required power due to the increase of blades sections angles of attack at the vortex ring state). Although this thrust drop should not be long-lasting, because while helicopter rotates on yaw the vortex ring state eliminates when angle between TR rotation axis and airflow velocity vector exceeds 40°. When the propeller is blown for decreasing blades sections angles of attack (in the direction against propeller thrust vector) in the range of angles between propeller axis of rotation and airflow velocity vector from 0° to 70° there is a monotonous decrease of propeller thrust. While being axial blowing with airflow velocity V 10 m/s the propeller thrust drops by 80% relative to the thrust without blowing, which may lead to the occurrence of unanticipated yaw rotation. Besides this, with this blowing direction, helicopter airframe
